NAME
       ausearch - search the audit log for events

SYNOPSIS
       ausearch [-m TYPE[,TYPE...]] [-c COMMAND] [-ts START]

DESCRIPTION
       The audit daemon (auditd) records security events in
       /var/log/audit/audit.log: logins, changes to accounts, and SELinux
       denials among them. Each record starts with its type and a time stamp:

           type=AVC msg=audit(1772442000.123:412): avc:  denied  { read } for
             pid=1234 comm="httpd" name="index.html" ...
             scontext=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0
             tcontext=unconfined_u:object_r:admin_home_t:s0 tclass=file

       ausearch picks out the records that match all the options you give.
       Matching records are printed as they are in the log, with a line of
       dashes between events. When nothing matches it prints <no matches>
       and exits with status 1.

       Only root can read the audit log.

   Reading an AVC denial
       denied { read }
              What the process tried to do.

       comm="httpd", pid=
              Which program did it.

       name=, path=
              The file (or other object) it touched.

       scontext
              The SELinux context of the process (here type httpd_t).

       tcontext
              The context of the target (here admin_home_t, a type the web
              server may not read: the file probably was moved from /root
              and kept its old label).

       tclass The kind of object: file, dir, tcp_socket...

OPTIONS
       -m TYPE, --message TYPE
              Only records of this type, for example AVC, USER_LOGIN,
              USER_AUTH, ADD_USER, SERVICE_START. Separate several types with
              commas.

       -c COMMAND, --comm COMMAND
              Only records caused by this program name (the comm= field).

       -ts START, --start START
              Only records from START on. recent means the last ten minutes,
              today means since midnight.

EXAMPLES
       Show the SELinux denials of the last ten minutes:

           ausearch -m AVC -ts recent

       Show today's denials caused by the web server:

           ausearch -m AVC -c httpd -ts today

       Pass the denials on to audit2why for an explanation:

           ausearch -m AVC -ts recent | audit2why

FILES
       /var/log/audit/audit.log
              The audit log.

NOTES
       If setroubleshoot-server is installed, sealert -a
       /var/log/audit/audit.log explains each denial in plain words and
       suggests commands to fix it. Also try journalctl -t setroubleshoot.

SEE ALSO
       audit2allow(1), sealert(8), auditd(8), journalctl(1)
